Tales of Ten Maidservants.
This is what the Kingdom of Heaven will be like at that time. Ten virgins came forward with their lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them were fools. Five were wise.
The foolish took their lamps, but did not take oil with them.
The wise ones took oil in jars along with their lamps.
The groom hasn't come for a long time. Therefore, all the bridesmaids felt bored and dozed off while lying down.
At midnight someone shouted, 'Here is the bridegroom! Come out to meet him!'
Then all the bridesmaids woke up and prepared their lamps.
The foolish ones said to the wise ones, 'Give us some of your oil. Our lamps are going out.'
"No," they answered. "There may not be enough for all of us. Instead, go to those who sell oil. Buy some for yourselves."
So they went to buy oil. But while they were on their way, the bridegroom arrived. And the bridesmaids, who were ready with him, went in to the wedding feast. Then the door was shut.
Later, the rest of the bridesmaids came. "Oh, my lord! My lord!" they cried. "Open the door for us!"
But he replied, "What I am going to tell you now is true. I do not know you."
So watch closely, for you do not know on which day or at which hour the bridegroom will come. A tale of three servants.
Again, here is what the Kingdom of Heaven will be like. A man was going on a journey. He sent for his servants and put them in charge of his possessions.
He gave ten thousand dollars to one. He gave four thousand dollars to another. And he gave two thousand dollars to the third. The man gave each servant the amount he knew the servant could take care of. Then he set out on his journey.
The servant who had received the $10,000 went at once and put his money to work. He earned $10,000 more.
The one with the $4,000 earned $4,000 more.
But the man who had received $2000 went and dug a hole in the ground. He hid his master's money in that hole.
After a long time, the master of those servants returned. He wanted to gather all the money that they had earned.
The man who had received $10,000 brought the other $10,000. 'Master,' he said, 'you trusted me with $10,000. See, I have earned $10,000 more.'
His master answered, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful over a few things; I will make you ruler over many things. Come and share your master's joy!"
The man who had four thousand dollars also came. He said, "Sir, you have entrusted me with four thousand dollars. Look, I have earned an additional four thousand dollars."
His master answered, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful over a few things; I will make you ruler over many things. Come and share your master's joy!"
Then the man to whom the two thousand dollars had been paid came. And he said, "Sir, I have learned that you are a hard man. You sow where you have not sown, and gather where you have not scattered."
So I was even afraid. So I went out and hid the two thousand dollars that belonged to you in the ground. Look, this is what belonged to you.
His master answered, "You wicked and lazy servant! So you knew that I sow where I did not plant, and I harvest where I did not scatter seed?"
Well then, you should have put my money in the bank. When I came back, I would have gotten it back again with interest.
Then his master ordered the other servants, 'Take the two thousand dollars from him. Give it to the one who has twenty thousand dollars.'
